{In this section, we consider applying the FEM to the following Poisson equation:}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poisson-equation}
-\Delta u(x,y)=f,\quad(x,y)\in\Omega,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poisson-dirichlet}
\cdot\ u(x,y)=g,\quad(x,y)\in\Gamma_1,\quad\text{Dirichlet Condition},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poisson-neumann}
\cdot\ \frac{\partial}{\partial n}u(x,y)=0,\quad(x,y)\in\Gamma_2,\quad\text{Neumann Condition},
\end{equation}
{where $\Delta:=\partial^2/\partial x^2+\partial^2/\partial y^2$. The domain and the boundary with the boundary conditions are illustrated in Fig.\ \ref{fig:poisson-domain}. In the following, the explanations are based on \BookCite{118}, \BookCite{170}.}

\subsubsection{{Weak Form}}\label{subsec:weak-form}
{In this section, an integral equation called the weak form is derived from the Poisson equation. Consider integrating \eqref{eq:poisson-equation} multiplied by an arbitrary function over the domain $\Omega$:}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poisson-weighted-integral}
-\int_\Omega v\Delta u\,\mathrm d\Omega=\int_\Omega vf\,\mathrm d\Omega.
\end{equation}
{If we can find a function $u$ that satisfies \eqref{eq:poisson-weighted-integral} for all $v$, then $u$ is the solution of \eqref{eq:poisson-equation}. The FEM tries to find the solution $u$ that satisfies \eqref{eq:poisson-weighted-integral} instead of solving \eqref{eq:poisson-equation}, which differs from the finite difference methods. Here, function $v$ is referred to as the test function or the weight function.}

{Now, in order to simplify the mathematical formulas, let $v$ be an arbitrary smooth function but $v=0$ on $\Gamma_1$. Applying the Gauss--Green theorem to the left-hand side of \eqref{eq:poisson-weighted-integral}, the integration by parts yields}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poisson-integration-by-parts}
\begin{aligned}
\int_\Omega v\Delta u\,\mathrm d\Omega
&=\int_\Gamma v\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}\,\mathrm d\Gamma-\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla u\,\mathrm d\Omega\quad\text{(the Gauss--Green theorem)}\\
&=\int_{\Gamma_1}v\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}\,\mathrm d\Gamma_1+\int_{\Gamma_2}v\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}\,\mathrm d\Gamma_2-\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla u\,\mathrm d\Omega\\
&=-\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla u\,\mathrm d\Omega.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
{Here we used the assumption $v=0$ on $\Gamma_1$ and Neumann condition \eqref{eq:poisson-neumann} on $\Gamma_2$, i.e., $\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}=0$ on $\Gamma_2$. Substituting \eqref{eq:poisson-integration-by-parts} into \eqref{eq:poisson-weighted-integral} gives}
\[\int_\Omega\nabla v\cdot\nabla u\,\mathrm d\Omega=\int_\Omega vf\,\mathrm d\Omega.\]
{Thus all we have to do is to find $u$ such that}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poisson-weak-form}
\iint_\Omega\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}\frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right)\mathrm dx\mathrm dy=\iint_\Omega vf\,\mathrm dx\mathrm dy.
\end{equation}
{The equation does not have the second-order derivative, whereas the original Eq.\ \eqref{eq:poisson-equation} does. In other words, the condition of differentiability of $u$ in \eqref{eq:poisson-weak-form} looks to be weak. Therefore, \eqref{eq:poisson-weak-form} is referred to as the weak form.}

{As seen in \eqref{eq:poisson-weak-form}, the weak form is the integral over the whole domain. Now consider that the whole domain is divided into finite elements $e_i$, i.e., $\Omega=\bigcup_{i=1}^n e_i$ and $e_i\cap e_j=\emptyset$ for $i\ne j$. Then we have}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:elementwise-poisson-weak-form}
\iint_{e_i}\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}\frac{\partial u}{\partial y}\right)\mathrm dx\mathrm dy=\iint_{e_i}vf\,\mathrm dx\mathrm dy
\end{equation}
{for $i=1,2,\ldots,n$. An example of the domain decomposition by triangle elements is shown in Fig.\ \ref{fig:triangular-domain-decomposition}. In the next section, we will see how linear systems are obtained from \eqref{eq:elementwise-poisson-weak-form}.}

{Now we introduce the following vector-valued function:}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:triangle-shape-functions}
\boldsymbol{h}(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}h_1(x,y)\\h_2(x,y)\\h_3(x,y)\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\x_1&x_2&x_3\\y_1&y_2&y_3\end{pmatrix}^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}1\\x\\y\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
{and let $\widetilde u$ be an approximate function to the solution function $u$ over the triangular domain such that $\widetilde u$ is a linear function of $x,y$ with $\widetilde u(x_i,y_i)=u_i$ for $i=1,2,3$. Then $\widetilde u$ is given by}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:triangle-linear-interpolant}
\widetilde u=\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}_e=(1\ x\ y)\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\x_1&x_2&x_3\\y_1&y_2&y_3\end{pmatrix}^{-\top}\begin{pmatrix}u_1\\u_2\\u_3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
{Confirming equalities $\widetilde u(x_i,y_i)=u_i$ for $1\le i\le3$ is left as an exercise for the reader.}

{Now substituting $\widetilde u$ into the left integrand in \eqref{eq:elementwise-poisson-weak-form} and choosing $v$ as the same form of $\widetilde u$, i.e., $v=\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}\boldsymbol{v}_e$ with $\boldsymbol{v}_e:=(v_1,v_2,v_3)^{\top}$ gives}
\[\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\frac{\partial\widetilde u}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}\frac{\partial\widetilde u}{\partial y}
&=\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\boldsymbol{h}\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}_e+\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\boldsymbol{h}\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}\boldsymbol{u}_e\\
&=\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\boldsymbol{h}\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}+\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\boldsymbol{h}\frac{\partial}{\partial y}\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}\right)\boldsymbol{u}_e,
\end{aligned}\]
{where we used the relation $\boldsymbol{h}^{\top}\boldsymbol{v}_e=\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\boldsymbol{h}$. Here, from \eqref{eq:triangle-shape-functions}, it follows that}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:triangle-shape-x-derivative}
\boldsymbol{h}_x:=\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{h}}{\partial x}
=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\x_1&x_2&x_3\\y_1&y_2&y_3\end{pmatrix}^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}0\\1\\0\end{pmatrix}
=\frac1D\begin{pmatrix}y_2-y_3\\y_3-y_1\\y_1-y_2\end{pmatrix}:=\begin{pmatrix}b_1\\b_2\\b_3\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:triangle-shape-y-derivative}
\boldsymbol{h}_y:=\frac{\partial\boldsymbol{h}}{\partial y}
=\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\x_1&x_2&x_3\\y_1&y_2&y_3\end{pmatrix}^{-1}\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}
=\frac1D\begin{pmatrix}x_3-x_2\\x_1-x_3\\x_2-x_1\end{pmatrix}:=\begin{pmatrix}c_1\\c_2\\c_3\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
{where $D$ is defined by}
\[D:=\det\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\x_1&x_2&x_3\\y_1&y_2&y_3\end{pmatrix}.\]

{$D$ is twice the area of the triangle. Since $\boldsymbol{h}_x$ and $\boldsymbol{h}_y$ do not depend on $x,y$, the left side of \eqref{eq:elementwise-poisson-weak-form} is calculated as follows:}
\[\begin{aligned}
\iint_{e_i}\left(\frac{\partial v}{\partial x}\frac{\partial\widetilde u}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial v}{\partial y}\frac{\partial\widetilde u}{\partial y}\right)\mathrm dx\mathrm dy
&=\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}(\boldsymbol{h}_x\boldsymbol{h}_x^{\top}+\boldsymbol{h}_y\boldsymbol{h}_y^{\top})\boldsymbol{u}_e\iint_{e_i}\mathrm dx\mathrm dy\\
&=\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\frac{(\boldsymbol{h}_x\boldsymbol{h}_x^{\top}+\boldsymbol{h}_y\boldsymbol{h}_y^{\top})D}{2}\boldsymbol{u}_e\\
&=\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}A_e\boldsymbol{u}_e.
\end{aligned}\]
{Here, $\iint_{e_i}\mathrm dx\mathrm dy$ is the area of triangle $e_i$, i.e., $D/2$. Matrix $A_e$ is a $3\times3$ symmetric matrix whose $(i,j)$ element, $a_{ij}^{(e)}$, is given by}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:element-stiffness-entry}
a_{ij}^{(e)}=\frac D2(b_ib_j+c_ic_j),
\end{equation}
{from \eqref{eq:triangle-shape-x-derivative} and \eqref{eq:triangle-shape-y-derivative}. The right-hand side of \eqref{eq:elementwise-poisson-weak-form} is written as}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:element-load-vector}
\boldsymbol{f}_e:=\begin{pmatrix}f_1^{(e)}\\f_2^{(e)}\\f_3^{(e)}\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}\iint_{e_i}f_0h_1\,\mathrm dx\mathrm dy\\\iint_{e_i}f_0h_2\,\mathrm dx\mathrm dy\\\iint_{e_i}f_0h_3\,\mathrm dx\mathrm dy\end{pmatrix}
=\frac{f_0D}{6}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\1\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
{where we used $f(x,y)=f_0$ (constant function) for simplicity. Then, we have}
\[\iint_{e_i}vf\,\mathrm dx\mathrm dy=\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\boldsymbol{f}_e.\]

{On calculating the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:element-load-vector}, the integrand $h_i$ ($1\le i\le3$) is a triangular pyramid with the height being 1 and the area of the base being $D/2$. Thus the volume of the triangle pyramid is $(D/2)\cdot1\cdot1/3=D/6$. From this, the left-hand side and the right-hand side of \eqref{eq:elementwise-poisson-weak-form} are approximated by}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:element-weak-matrix-pair}
\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}A_e\boldsymbol{u}_e,\quad\boldsymbol{v}_e^{\top}\boldsymbol{f}_e.
\end{equation}
{The above process is the derivation of \eqref{eq:element-weak-matrix-pair} regarding a small area $e_i$. Considering all the linear systems for all $e_i$'s together with boundary conditions leads to large linear systems. By solving the large linear systems, the approximate solution $u_i$ on each node can be obtained. In the next subsection, we will see how large linear systems can be obtained.}

\subsubsection{Example}\label{subsec:finite-element-example}
{As an example of the previous subsection, we consider Poisson Eq.\ \eqref{eq:poisson-equation} on the $(0,1)\times(0,1)$ square domain $\Omega$ in Fig.\ \ref{fig:square-poisson-domain}, and we will see how linear systems are obtained.}
{We first decompose the square domain in Fig.\ \ref{fig:square-poisson-domain} into eight triangles in Fig.\ \ref{fig:eight-triangle-mesh}. Next, calculate \eqref{eq:element-weak-matrix-pair} for $A_e$ and $\boldsymbol{f}_e$ in each triangle element in Fig.\ \ref{fig:eight-triangle-mesh}, and finally, combine all the information, leading to linear systems.}
{In what follows, we calculate \eqref{eq:element-weak-matrix-pair} for $A_e$ and $\boldsymbol{f}_e$. We see from Fig.\ \ref{fig:eight-triangle-mesh} that there are two kinds of triangles, i.e., type I for $e_1^{\mathrm I},e_3^{\mathrm I},e_5^{\mathrm I},e_7^{\mathrm I}$ and type II for $e_2^{\mathrm {II}},e_4^{\mathrm {II}},e_6^{\mathrm {II}},e_8^{\mathrm {II}}$, leading to two types of linear systems corresponding to \eqref{eq:laplace-five-point}. The details of the two triangles are shown in Fig.\ \ref{fig:two-triangle-types}.}

{For the type I triangle, it follows from \eqref{eq:triangle-shape-x-derivative}, \eqref{eq:triangle-shape-y-derivative}, and recalling $D$ being twice the area of the triangle (i.e., $D=h^2$), we have}
\[\begin{pmatrix}b_1\\b_2\\b_3\end{pmatrix}=\frac1{h^2}\begin{pmatrix}-h\\h\\0\end{pmatrix},\quad
\begin{pmatrix}c_1\\c_2\\c_3\end{pmatrix}=\frac1{h^2}\begin{pmatrix}-h\\0\\h\end{pmatrix}.\]
{Then, from \eqref{eq:element-stiffness-entry} and \eqref{eq:element-load-vector}, we obtain}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:type-one-element-matrix}
A_{e^{\mathrm I}}=\frac12\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&-1\\-1&1&0\\-1&0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
\boldsymbol{f}_{e^{\mathrm I}}=\frac{f_0h^2}{6}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\\1\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
{which correspond to \eqref{eq:element-weak-matrix-pair}. Similarly, for the type II triangle, we have}
\[\begin{pmatrix}b_1\\b_2\\b_3\end{pmatrix}=\frac1{h^2}\begin{pmatrix}0\\h\\-h\end{pmatrix},\quad
\begin{pmatrix}c_1\\c_2\\c_3\end{pmatrix}=\frac1{h^2}\begin{pmatrix}-h\\h\\0\end{pmatrix},\]
{and thus}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:type-two-element-matrix}
